    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper evaluates held-out WikiText-fr perplexity for models with different parameter counts, but does not fit or test a scaling law. This is a cross-model measurement rather than a controlled scaling sweep.",
    "gate_note": "Table 2 reports byte-level and token-level perplexity for several differently sized models on WikiText-fr, so those measurements bear on V1. The study does not vary parameter count, tokens, or compute in a controlled setup, fit power laws, or test extrapolation or compute-optimal allocation. Its downstream benchmark tables compare model scores but do not test predictable downstream scaling. Cited scaling-law results are not this study’s own results.",
    "test_reason": "The paper cites scaling laws to motivate larger models but evaluates a French model rather than testing any listed scaling relationship. Table 2 reports perplexities across heterogeneous models without establishing power-law dependence on parameters, data or compute. Downstream comparisons and the supplementary training experiments likewise do not test scaling predictability.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record correctly states that Cedille has the lowest WikiText-fr perplexity among the evaluated models and that the authors do not establish a scaling law. Their broader conclusion is that Cedille is competitive with GPT-3 on French zero-shot tasks and that training-data filtering reduces measured toxicity; they caution that downstream scores depend strongly on evaluation settings.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Cross-model zero-shot perplexity evaluation on WikiText-fr; no fitted scaling-law procedure or controlled size/data/compute sweep.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Context length differs across model groups, and the authors note token-level perplexity comparability is affected by tokenizer differences.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Table 2 compares French perplexity across existing models; Cedille has the lowest reported byte and token perplexities, 1.646 and 3.932. Context lengths and tokenizers differ, and the authors warn of likely training overlap with the test data.",
        "proposed_claim": "French continued pretraining can yield competitive French perplexity in a smaller model relative to larger multilingual models.",
